# app.js has no content hash in its filename - every deploy overwrites it at
# the same URL. Without this, a browser (or an intermediate cache) can keep
# serving a stale copy for a while after a new deploy, so a tab open across
# two deploys can end up running JS from one version against HTML from
# another - exactly the kind of mismatch that throws "Cannot set properties
# of null" on an element the newer JS expects and the older HTML doesn't have,
# or the other way round. Forcing revalidation on every load costs nothing
# here; this is a small app shell, not an asset-heavy site.
#
# The firmware binaries under /firmware/ are NOT covered by this - those are
# genuinely large and content-addressed by version+env in the path already,
# so the platform's default caching for them is fine.
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/app.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/update.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache
